This document licenses the source code of Ecoma. It is a fair-code licence:
the source is public, and you may read it, self-host it and modify it. It is
not an OSI open-source licence, and Ecoma is never described as "open source".
What it withholds is commercial redistribution — above all, selling Ecoma
itself as a service, and any distribution to others that is not both free of
charge and non-commercial. That restriction is why the rest can be open.

That paragraph describes the licence in outline. The sections below are its
terms, and where the two differ, the terms below govern.

These terms are BASED ON the Sustainable Use License 1.0 but are not that
licence. The SCOPE section, the clarifications in LIMITATIONS, the patent
retaliation trigger, the definition of "the software", and the GOVERNING LAW,
LANGUAGE and LIABILITY sections are additions or changes. A review of the base
licence is not a review of this one.


SCOPE

These terms cover every file in the repository the licensor publishes at
https://github.com/ecoma-io/ecoma (the "Repository"), with five exceptions.

Each exception identifies files by their path within the Repository as the
licensor publishes it. The terms that govern a file are fixed by its path in
the version of the Repository in which you received it. If the licensor later
publishes a version in which that file sits elsewhere, the terms of that later
version apply only to copies you obtain from it; no license you already hold is
narrowed or withdrawn. Moving, copying or renaming a file yourself changes
nothing. A file that is a copy of or a derivative work based on a file from the
Repository carries the same terms as its source file, regardless of its name,
location or the name of the directory it sits in within your own tree. This
paragraph governs over any other in this SCOPE section.

Where more than one exception could apply to a file, the later-numbered
exception prevails over the earlier.

  1. Any directory named `packages` directly beneath a subsystem root — which
     the licensor uses only for the interfaces, schemas, protocols, clients
     and vocabularies a third party needs in order to plug into Ecoma — is
     licensed by the licensor under the Apache License 2.0, published at
     https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0 and also placed in that
     directory. Nothing in this document limits those files. If such a
     directory reaches you without a copy of the Apache License 2.0 in it, the
     Apache License 2.0 governs those files nonetheless.

  2. Any directory named `enterprise` directly beneath a subsystem root is NOT
     licensed by this document. Those files are published so that they can be
     read and audited, and no right to use, copy, distribute, make available
     or prepare derivative works of them is granted here. Using them requires
     a separate written Enterprise License from the licensor, whose terms are
     placed in that directory. If those terms are not present, no rights in
     those files are granted at all.

  3. Every file whose name ends in `.md`, in `shared/libs/doctrine/` and in
     every directory beneath it, together with every image file located in
     that directory or beneath it that such a file references, is licensed by
     the licensor under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
     International License, whose full text is at
     https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode and which is
     summarised in `shared/libs/doctrine/LICENSE.docs`. An image outside that
     directory is not licensed by this exception, whatever references it.
     Every other file in that directory, including everything under
     `shared/libs/doctrine/src/`, is covered by this document. If such a file
     reaches you without a copy of `LICENSE.docs`, that Creative Commons
     licence governs it nonetheless.

  4. The `cloud/` directory is proprietary. Its contents are not published in
     the Repository. If it is present in your working tree, it is not licensed
     by this document and no rights to it are granted here.

  5. Any third-party component distributed in the Repository remains licensed
     under the terms its own owner provides, which are stated in or alongside
     that component. The licensor grants no rights in such components beyond
     those their owner grants, and nothing in this document limits or extends
     them.


